 Water District"}],"more":0},{"year":2001,"tests":[],"violations":[{"y":2001,"contaminant":"Consumer Confidence Rule","health":false,"cat":"Other","measure":null,"unit":null,"limit":null,"from":"2001-07-01","to":null,"resolved":true,"system":"Elm Hill Water District"}],"more":0}],"first_year":2001,"last_year":2025,"explain":{"Arsenic":{"plain":"Arsenic occurs naturally in bedrock across much of New England \u2014 it is not usually a pollution problem, it is a geology problem. It is especially common in granite country.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing arsenic in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could experience skin damage or problems with their circulatory system, and may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Arsenic","who":"Risk builds over years of exposure rather than suddenly.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Arsenic"},"Copper":{"plain":"Copper, like lead, usually comes from the plumbing rather than the source \u2014 most often from copper pipe and its fittings, especially where water sits still.","effect":"Copper is an essential nutrient, but some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over a relatively short amount of time could experience gastrointestinal distress. Some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over many years could suffer liver or kidney damage.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"People with Wilson's disease should consult their doctor.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Copper"},"Benzene":{"plain":"Benzene is an industrial solvent, and also reaches groundwater from leaking fuel storage tanks and landfills.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing benzene in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could experience anemia or a decrease in blood platelets, and may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Benzene","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Benzene"},"Lead":{"plain":"Lead is almost never in water at its source. It gets picked up on the way to the tap \u2014 from the service line under the street, from solder in older joints, from the brass in the tap itself. That makes it a property of your building more than of your town.","effect":"Infants and children who drink water containing lead in excess of the action level could experience delays in their physical or mental development. Children could show slight deficits in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"Lead and Copper Rule":{"kind":"rule","name":"Lead and Copper Rule","plain":"The rule governing lead and copper monitoring and corrosion control. A violation is often procedural \u2014 a missed sampling round or a late report.","effect":null,"source":null,"who":null}}}